00:30:21.430 --> 00:30:24.440
Dave, we got a bunch of great
questions from the audience.

00:30:24.440 --> 00:30:26.870
Our moderator has popped
those into a slide for us

00:30:26.870 --> 00:30:28.310
to be able to see.

00:30:28.310 --> 00:30:30.600
We get a ton of great
questions over time.

00:30:30.600 --> 00:30:32.550
This is actually a pretty
common one as well.

00:30:32.550 --> 00:30:35.240
The first one how do people
join different data sets

00:30:35.240 --> 00:30:36.120
in Data Studio?

00:30:36.120 --> 00:30:39.710
DAVE OLESON: So right now, we
don't have support for joins

00:30:39.710 --> 00:30:40.570
within Data Studio.

00:30:40.570 --> 00:30:42.560
This is something we're
actively working on,

00:30:42.560 --> 00:30:44.630
so look for this
in the near future.

00:30:44.630 --> 00:30:47.630
The workaround right now is to
pump your data into BigQuery

00:30:47.630 --> 00:30:49.479
and do the join actually
inside of BigQuery

00:30:49.479 --> 00:30:51.395
and then visualize that
inside of Data Studio.

00:30:51.395 --> 00:30:52.800
SOLEIL KELLEY: What would
I need to be able to do

00:30:52.800 --> 00:30:53.750
within BigQuery to do that?

00:30:53.750 --> 00:30:55.999
DAVE OLESON: So just write
a simple SQL join statement

00:30:55.999 --> 00:30:56.885
in BigQuery.

00:30:56.885 --> 00:30:58.550
SOLEIL KELLEY: Great.

00:30:58.550 --> 00:31:01.292
How about viewing dashboards
on mobile devices?

00:31:01.292 --> 00:31:02.000
DAVE OLESON: Yes.

00:31:02.000 --> 00:31:03.890
So you can view dashboards
on mobile devices.

00:31:03.890 --> 00:31:05.681
You just have to scale
them down because we

00:31:05.681 --> 00:31:08.630
offer a lot of flexibility in
what you can put on the page.

00:31:08.630 --> 00:31:10.730
So obviously you can't
have as much granularity

00:31:10.730 --> 00:31:11.801
on a mobile device.

00:31:11.801 --> 00:31:13.550
So what we've seen
people do is scale down

00:31:13.550 --> 00:31:15.470
the number of controls
that are on the page

00:31:15.470 --> 00:31:17.276
to be able to
visualize and access

00:31:17.276 --> 00:31:18.400
that information on mobile.

00:31:18.400 --> 00:31:20.750
SOLEIL KELLEY: That
makes a ton of sense.

00:31:20.750 --> 00:31:23.150
And then again using
this internally,

00:31:23.150 --> 00:31:24.810
what access control
features does

00:31:24.810 --> 00:31:26.540
Data Studio support
for dashboards

00:31:26.540 --> 00:31:27.830
and also the underlying data?

00:31:27.830 --> 00:31:29.330
Where does that data reside?

00:31:29.330 --> 00:31:31.100
DAVE OLESON: So the
underlying data always

00:31:31.100 --> 00:31:32.641
relies on the
underlying data system,

00:31:32.641 --> 00:31:36.080
and there's multiple off
models that we support.

00:31:36.080 --> 00:31:38.070
So Data Studio is built
on top of Google Drive.

00:31:38.070 --> 00:31:42.200
So-- versus like you'd share
a sheet or a Google doc.

00:31:42.200 --> 00:31:44.750
That's how we-- you would share
a Google Data Studio report.

00:31:44.750 --> 00:31:46.880
Now for the underlying
data, there's

00:31:46.880 --> 00:31:48.950
different access
models for connecting

00:31:48.950 --> 00:31:52.010
to the data using both owner's
credentials and viewer's

00:31:52.010 --> 00:31:52.550
credentials.

00:31:52.550 --> 00:31:55.130
So if you set viewer's
credentials on a data source,

00:31:55.130 --> 00:31:56.750
then everyone who has access--

00:31:56.750 --> 00:31:59.112
everyone who wants to
render that data in a report

00:31:59.112 --> 00:32:01.070
will have to have access
to the underlying data

00:32:01.070 --> 00:32:03.278
because they will be using
their credentials to query

00:32:03.278 --> 00:32:04.221
the underlying system.

00:32:04.221 --> 00:32:05.345
SOLEIL KELLEY: Makes sense.

00:32:05.345 --> 00:32:05.845
Cool.

00:32:05.845 --> 00:32:07.460
Thank you.

00:32:07.460 --> 00:32:09.970
And then what about connecting
to external data sources?

00:32:09.970 --> 00:32:11.720
We brought this up in
the slides actually.

00:32:11.720 --> 00:32:12.140
What is it?

00:32:12.140 --> 00:32:12.680
DAVE OLESON: Sure, yeah.

00:32:12.680 --> 00:32:14.027
So we connect to a lot of--

00:32:14.027 --> 00:32:15.110
this is a common question.

00:32:15.110 --> 00:32:17.470
We connect to a lot of
Google data sources--

00:32:17.470 --> 00:32:19.370
Google ads data and
Google cloud data.

00:32:19.370 --> 00:32:22.300
We also connect to on prem
data like MySQL and Postgres.

00:32:22.300 --> 00:32:23.050
And then we have--

00:32:23.050 --> 00:32:26.150
we're growing an ecosystem to
connect to all sorts of data

00:32:26.150 --> 00:32:27.120
around the web.

00:32:27.120 --> 00:32:30.634
So we have over 300 connections
to different data sources

00:32:30.634 --> 00:32:32.550
around the web, and
that's constantly growing.

00:32:32.550 --> 00:32:35.120
So if we don't have
it, you actually

00:32:35.120 --> 00:32:36.590
can go and build it yourself.

00:32:36.590 --> 00:32:39.650
We've had our UX team.

00:32:39.650 --> 00:32:43.914
Even our director has built
simple connectors and prototype

00:32:43.914 --> 00:32:45.080
connectors in under an hour.

00:32:45.080 --> 00:32:46.065
SOLEIL KELLEY: Cool.

00:32:46.065 --> 00:32:47.030
That's great.

00:32:47.030 --> 00:32:49.610
And then talk a little bit
more about how filters work

00:32:49.610 --> 00:32:51.931
and the different elements
that you can filter by.

00:32:51.931 --> 00:32:52.680
DAVE OLESON: Sure.

00:32:52.680 --> 00:32:53.180
Yes.

00:32:53.180 --> 00:32:54.854
So we have multiple
types of filters.

00:32:54.854 --> 00:32:56.270
So there's filters
which I showed,

00:32:56.270 --> 00:32:59.210
which are on the page, which
you can have at the page level,

00:32:59.210 --> 00:33:00.920
at the report level,
or you can even

00:33:00.920 --> 00:33:02.220
buy into specific elements.

00:33:02.220 --> 00:33:04.547
So I could say, I only want
to filter this one graph

00:33:04.547 --> 00:33:06.380
by department, and then
I want to compare it

00:33:06.380 --> 00:33:08.600
against my overall
revenue in another chart.

00:33:08.600 --> 00:33:12.440
We also have filters that exist
at what we call the report

00:33:12.440 --> 00:33:13.790
level or the editor level.

00:33:13.790 --> 00:33:15.380
So the editors sets
those, and they

00:33:15.380 --> 00:33:22.070
support red jacks,
exclusion, end filters,

00:33:22.070 --> 00:33:24.512
greater than or less than
operators for metric filters,

00:33:24.512 --> 00:33:25.970
and then we also
have ands and ors.

00:33:25.970 --> 00:33:28.011
So there's a lot of
flexibility in our filtering.

00:33:28.011 --> 00:33:29.960
SOLEIL KELLEY: Great.

00:33:29.960 --> 00:33:34.544
And then finally, exporting to
PDF, tell us more about this.

00:33:34.544 --> 00:33:35.460
DAVE OLESON: You can--

00:33:35.460 --> 00:33:37.220
this is a common request.

00:33:37.220 --> 00:33:39.340
There is a single
page export to PDF.

00:33:39.340 --> 00:33:42.830
If you are in Chrome, you
can use the file print

00:33:42.830 --> 00:33:43.497
to print to PDF.

00:33:43.497 --> 00:33:45.163
And this is something
that we're looking

00:33:45.163 --> 00:33:46.325
